---
registryId: consistent-hashing
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is consistent hashing?

**Consistent hashing is a placement technique for choosing an owner from a changing
set of nodes.** It hashes both keys and node positions into the same ordered space.
A key belongs to the first node position reached while walking around that space.

Its core invariant is **limited movement during membership change**. Adding or
removing one node should transfer only the ranges whose owner changed, rather than
recomputing almost every key as `hash(key) % nodeCount` usually does.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Consistent hashing answers **where a key should go**. It does not copy data, reach
consensus, detect failures, move bytes, or make a write strongly consistent. Those
behaviors need separate replication, membership, streaming, and consistency protocols.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Put keys and owners in one ordered space

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Hash space" eyebrow="Stable coordinate system" icon="hash" tone="blue" %}
A deterministic hash turns each key and node identity into a position. The production
hash space is large; lesson diagrams use a small circle so ownership is visible.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Token" eyebrow="Node position" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
A token is one position owned by a physical node. One node may own many tokens, which
split its responsibility into smaller ranges.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Successor owner" eyebrow="Clockwise lookup" icon="route" tone="amber" %}
Hash the key, find the first token at or after that position, and wrap to the first
token when the lookup passes the end of the space.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Token range" eyebrow="Movement unit" icon="layers" tone="green" %}
The interval after one token and through the next token shares an owner. Membership
change transfers ranges, not arbitrary individual keys.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% system-flow title="One consistent-hash lookup" caption="Every participant must use the same hash, token map, and ownership direction for the result to be deterministic." %}
{% system-node title="Stable key" eyebrow="Identity" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
Choose the business identity that must keep the same owner, such as a cache key,
tenant ID, or session ID.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Hash position" eyebrow="Coordinate" icon="hash" tone="violet" %}
Apply the agreed hash function and map the result into the ring's numeric range.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="First token clockwise" eyebrow="Lookup" icon="route" tone="amber" %}
Use a sorted token map and a successor search. Wrap around when no larger token exists.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Physical owner" eyebrow="Route" icon="server" tone="green" %}
Send the request to the node behind that token, or begin a replica walk when the
system stores more than one copy.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## See what moves when membership changes

The lab uses the same 72 deterministic key hashes for every comparison. Change the
number of virtual positions, then add node D or remove node C. The before and after
bars show measured ownership of this fixture, while the modulo baseline shows how many
of those exact keys would change owners under `hash % nodeCount`.

{% interactive-block id="technology/consistent-hashing-calculator" dataFile="/api/content/technology/consistent-hashing/data/membership-movement-model.json" /%}

### Read the result correctly

- The moved percentage is an observation from the fixed sample, not a universal
  promise for every key distribution.
- Adding one equally weighted node to `N` balanced nodes tends toward moving about
  `1 / (N + 1)` of keys to the new node.
- Removing one balanced node tends toward moving that node's prior share.
- More virtual positions usually reduce ownership variance, but they add token-map,
  maintenance, and failure-neighbor overhead.
- Weighted placement should be modeled through explicit capacity weights and measured
  distribution, not by assuming every physical node is identical.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Implement the lookup as a small deterministic contract

The essential data structure is a sorted token list. A binary successor search makes
lookup `O(log T)` for `T` token positions. Membership updates can be less frequent and
more expensive, but every client or coordinator must converge on the same token map
before routing safely.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/consistent-hashing/code/hash-ring.py" language="python" title="A compact consistent-hash ring with virtual positions" /%}

### Treat membership as a control-plane change

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Publish the new token map" label="Version" %}
Give the membership view an epoch or version so stale routers can be detected instead
of silently disagreeing about ownership.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Stream the changing ranges" label="Transfer" %}
Copy or rebuild the affected data while the old owner can still serve it. Bound
bandwidth so rebalancing does not consume the live request budget.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Switch authoritative routing" label="Cut over" %}
Move reads and writes under an explicit handoff contract. Define how concurrent writes,
retries, and a failed transfer are reconciled.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Retire the old ownership" label="Verify" %}
Delete old copies only after integrity, replica count, and request routing are proven
for the new map.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Walk replicas across distinct failure boundaries

A ring can nominate several tokens for one key, but several adjacent tokens may belong
to the same physical node. Counting those token positions as independent replicas
creates false durability. Production storage systems therefore skip duplicate physical
owners, and topology-aware strategies can also spread replicas across racks or
datacenters.

Use the lab to change the replication factor, placement policy, key position, and
failure. The trace shows which tokens are selected or skipped and whether the remaining
copies survive the injected boundary.

{% interactive-block id="technology/consistent-hashing-replica-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/consistent-hashing/data/replica-failure-model.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/consistent-hashing/code/replica-walk.py" language="python" title="Skip duplicate nodes and failure domains during replica selection" /%}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Replication factor is not a consistency level. Three placed copies do not say how many
must acknowledge a write, how reads reconcile versions, or what happens during a
partition. Define those rules independently.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Choose the placement family deliberately

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Ring-based consistent hashing" eyebrow="Elastic key ownership" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Use a ring when keys or requests need stable affinity while a node set changes and the
system can operate token metadata plus controlled movement.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Fixed hash slots" eyebrow="Explicit resharding" icon="database" tone="blue" %}
Use a fixed slot space when operators should move named slot ranges explicitly.
Redis Cluster uses 16,384 hash slots and states that it does **not** use consistent
hashing.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Round robin or least request" eyebrow="Stateless balancing" icon="route" tone="amber" %}
Use ordinary load balancing when requests do not need key affinity. It can respond to
live load more directly than a deterministic hash.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Do not reach for a ring when the real requirement is different

- Use range partitioning when ordered scans and adjacent-key locality dominate.
- Use a partition directory when placement must reflect changing, explicit metadata.
- Use rendezvous or jump hashing when their membership and storage contracts fit
  better than maintaining a token ring.
- Use a topology-aware database strategy rather than a home-grown ring when the system
  also needs repair, replica consistency, failure detection, and safe rebalancing.
- Treat a hot key separately. Virtual nodes improve aggregate ownership distribution;
  they do not split one key across owners.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Operate the movement, not only the hash function

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="Range share" label="Ownership balance" detail="Bytes, keys, and requests per physical node" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="Moved bytes" label="Membership cost" detail="Transfer rate, duration, retries, and foreground impact" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="Map epoch" label="Routing agreement" detail="Stale clients, rejected versions, and ownership mismatches" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="Replica health" label="Failure coverage" detail="Distinct nodes and failure domains for every range" tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Production checklist

- Choose the routing key from the affinity contract and document how multi-key
  operations behave.
- Use a stable, well-tested hash implementation across languages and versions.
- Measure key count, bytes, request rate, and hot-key skew by owner; none alone proves
  balance.
- Version membership and define behavior for stale routers during a rollout.
- Throttle range movement and reserve foreground CPU, disk, and network capacity.
- Place replicas on distinct physical nodes and, where required, distinct failure
  domains.
- Exercise node join, graceful removal, abrupt loss, partial transfer, stale
  membership, and rollback.
- Verify ownership and checksums before deleting the prior copy of a moved range.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Verify the claims against primary references

- The original [ACM consistent hashing paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/258533.258660)
  defines the technique in the context of changing cache populations.
- Apache Cassandra's current [Dynamo architecture documentation](https://cassandra.apache.org/doc/stable/cassandra/architecture/dynamo.html)
  explains token rings, virtual nodes, distinct physical replicas, and topology-aware
  placement.
- Cassandra's [production token guidance](https://cassandra.apache.org/doc/stable/cassandra/getting-started/production.html)
  describes the balance between expansion flexibility, streaming peers, and
  availability.
- Envoy's current [ring-hash load-balancer documentation](https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/load_balancing/load_balancers.html)
  documents host weights, ring sizing, and the expected `1 / N` disruption for one
  host change.
- Redis documents its different [fixed hash-slot model](https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/scaling/)
  explicitly, including the 16,384-slot key space and operator-driven resharding.
{% /section-card %}

